\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{Ground Truth Mismatch}
\label{sec:groundtruth}

Direct comparison of DIF flags against the web-overlap labels of \citet{li2024contamination} on 9,229 annotated items confirms that the two methods capture orthogonal signals. The enrichment ratio is 0.964 (\Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}): DIF-C rates are 30.7\% among web-contaminated items and 31.8\% among clean items. Association tests yield $\phi = 0.012$, $\chi^2 = 1.28$ ($p = 0.258$), and OR $= 1.05$ [0.96, 1.15]; the Jaccard index between flagged sets is 0.206. This near-independence holds across contamination subtypes (input-only enrichment $= 0.986$, input-and-label $= 0.960$). The null result does not indicate detection failure: semi-synthetic injection produces a clear dose-response ($\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = {+}0.508$ at $p = 0.5$; \Cref{fig:dose_response}). The disconnect arises because web-overlap labels measure \emph{global exposure} (whether an item exists in training corpora), while DIF measures \emph{differential functioning} (whether an item's relationship to ability changes across cohorts). The Li et al.\ labels are the wrong ground truth for a differential method. This near-independence extends to behavioral contamination detectors: the enrichment ratio of $0.964$ and Jaccard overlap of $0.206$ between DIF flags and web-overlap labels imply that any detector validated against these labels---including ConStat~\citep{dekoninck2024constat}---is evaluated on a ground truth orthogonal to differential functioning.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_2_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Semi-synthetic dose-response for DIF detection on MMLU. $\Delta\mathrm{TPR}$ increases monotonically with exploitation rate $p$, confirming that MH-DIF is sensitive to differential item behavior when present. Baseline (no injection) produces $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} \approx 0$.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Structural Analysis}
\label{sec:structural}

Three structural controls fail to reduce temporal C\% below 31\%: matched-ability quintiles, IRT $\theta$-conditioning ($r(\theta, \mathrm{total\ score}) = 0.9986$, FPR $= 0.312$), and within-subject DIF (C\% $= 38.1\%$, exceeding the 25\% bifactor viability threshold). Logistic regression DIF confirms 92.1\% concordance with MH and reveals additional non-uniform DIF (24.8\% at $|\beta_{\mathrm{interaction}}| \geq 0.5$), making the 31\% estimate conservative. Graduated de-duplication ($K{=}10$: 22.7\% vs.\ random 26.8\%; $K{=}20$: 29.1\% vs.\ 30.9\%) confirms family structure does not inflate C\%. Ability-matched within-family analysis reduces Llama-2 vs.\ Llama-3 C\% from 61.3\% to $28.3\% \pm 1.6\%$ (Cohen's $d = -0.99$, overlap $= 0.18$), while temporal matching reduces C\% from 31.3\% to $28.9\% \pm 0.1\%$ ($d = -0.17$, overlap $= 0.76$). The matched C\% values ($\sim$28--29\%) are consistent with each other despite vastly different unmatched rates (61.3\% vs.\ 31.3\%), suggesting that genuine item-level DIF accounts for $\sim$28--29\% of items, with the Llama comparison carrying 33 pp of additional ability confound (54\% reduction) versus only 2.4 pp for temporal cohorts (7.7\% reduction). In total, six alternative explanations were tested and ruled out: ability confounding, model family inflation, IRT $\theta$-conditioning, within-subject effects, aggregate ranking impact, and local item dependence---none reduces C\% below $\sim$28\%, confirming item-level differential functioning. Full details in \Cref{app:structural}.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Framework Validation}
\label{sec:semisynthetic}

The critical methodological finding is that the choice of matching strategy determines validity. Cross-subject matching computes the conditioning variable from subjects \emph{other than} the one being tested, achieving $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0.000$ across all five MMLU subjects while four of five produce $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} > 0.3$ at $p = 0.5$. Within-subject matching inflates FPR by ${+}0.186$ on average because the matching variable absorbs part of the injection signal. We adopt cross-subject matching as the standard protocol (\Cref{app:fig_matching}).

\subsection{Downstream Impact}
\label{sec:downstream}

Spearman correlation between full and stable-item rankings is $\rho \approx 0.997$: aggregate model ordering is preserved despite 31\% item instability. DIF-flagged items nonetheless carry more discriminative signal than average ($z = -8\sigma$ to $-49\sigma$ vs.\ random removal across 21 conditions), though a circularity ablation shows DIF-based removal is the \emph{least} disruptive among non-random strategies (Jaccard overlap with high-discrimination items is only 0.12). Individual model families show distinctive DIF profiles: Llama-3 derivatives gain a median of ${+}53$ rank positions (mean ${+}121$), while Mistral-based models lose a median of $35$ (mean $-54$); individual extremes reach ${+}793$ and $-542$ (\Cref{app:fig_downstream}). Overall, 25\% of models shift by $|\Delta\mathrm{Rank}| \geq 100$. \Cref{sec:gsm8k} demonstrates that this instability pattern extends across all six METABENCH benchmarks.

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation}
\label{sec:gsm8k}

Temporal DIF extends across all six METABENCH benchmarks (\Cref{tab:cross_benchmark}). C\% ranges from 17.9\% (HellaSwag) to 31.3\% (MMLU), each 45--174$\times$ above its random-split baseline---none attributable to sampling noise. A task-type dichotomy emerges: knowledge and reasoning benchmarks (MMLU, WinoGrande~\citep{sakaguchi2021winogrande}, TruthfulQA~\citep{lin2022truthfulqa}, ARC~\citep{clark2018arc}) show C\% $= 26.9$--$31.3\%$, while procedural benchmarks (GSM8K, HellaSwag~\citep{zellers2019hellaswag}) show C\% $= 17.9$--$18.9\%$, consistent with knowledge-intensive items being more sensitive to shifts in training data composition across model generations.

